            <ArticleTitle>Alprazolam in the treatment of cardiology patients with atypical chest pain and panic disorder.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>127-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ten cardiology chest pain patients without clinical evidence of coronary artery disease and with panic disorder were enrolled in an open label trial of alprazolam. Two dropped out at week 3 because of excessive side effects. The eight study completers showed significant decreases in both Hamilton Anxiety and Hamilton Depression scales by week 2 that remained significant by week 8. On the physician's global impression of change since baseline, all completers were rated at least &quot;much improved.&quot; Seven experienced a 50% or greater reduction in panic frequency at week 8 compared to baseline. These results suggest that alprazolam is likely to be of benefit to this patient population.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
